<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0-windows</TargetFramework>
    <Platforms>x64</Platforms>
    <PlatformTarget>x64</PlatformTarget>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <RootNamespace>SmartLinkSessionHarness</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>SmartLinkSessionHarness</AssemblyName>
    <UseWindowsForms>true</UseWindowsForms>
    <UseWpf>true</UseWpf>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>

    <!--
      Task #135: RID-specific, so NuGet's whole cross-platform runtimes tree
      stops coming along.

      Without a RuntimeIdentifier the build cannot know which platform it is
      for, so it copies every native it might ever need. That put SIXTEEN
      System.IO.Ports natives in a tester's zip that can never execute on
      Windows — android-arm/arm64/x64/x86, four flavours of linux .so,
      maccatalyst, and osx .dylib.

      This is about CREDIBILITY, not bytes; it is only about 430 KB. Shipping
      Android libraries to a Windows ham radio tester invites "what else is in
      here that should not be?", and that is a fair question to be asked once
      and a bad one to keep earning.

      Framework-dependent, stated rather than inferred: the harness is a
      diagnostic tool that rides along inside the app's own self-contained
      publish tree, and a second copy of the runtime would add ~180 MB to
      every nightly.

      The RID appends to the output path, matching the main app's shape
      (bin\x64\Debug\net10.0-windows\win-x64\). build-debug.bat and this
      tool's README name that path.
    -->
    <RuntimeIdentifier>win-x64</RuntimeIdentifier>
    <SelfContained>false</SelfContained>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Radios\Radios.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\FlexLib_API\FlexLib\FlexLib.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\JJTrace\JJTrace.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
